Thanks, Tessa — this is helpful. A couple quick clarifying questions from our engineering lead: 

1) Peak TPS expectation: during a beta weekend we expect brief spikes to ~120 rps across our cluster. Will the recommended client-side token bucket be enough, or should we set up server-side rate limiting/routing?
2) Retry semantics: what error codes should we treat as safe to retry (and with what backoff)? We have a mix of idempotent and non-idempotent calls.
3) Do you provide test API keys that are scoped to a sandbox project and separate quotas?

Also looping in Priya (PM) to confirm rollout priorities. We can commit to a kickoff on 7/20 but need the SDK examples to land in a repo we can fork.

—Alex

On Tue, Jul 14, 2026 at 09:20 Marissa Cole <marissa_cole@redwood.ai> wrote:
> Hi Alex —
>
> In short: yes, the client-side token bucket + conservative concurrency is usually sufficient for short bursts. For sustained spikes above ~80 rps we recommend enabling a server-side ingress throttle (we can provision per-tenant throttle rules in Dedicated).
>
> Retry guidance (opinionated defaults we use in field):
> - 429: safe to retry with jittered exponential backoff (start 200ms, max 3s, 4 attempts)
> - 5xx: conditional retry for idempotent ops only (same backoff, 3 attempts)
> - 4xx (other than 429): do not retry (report and surface to caller)
>
> For non-idempotent calls: push the retry responsibility to the server or use an idempotency key (we include idempotency-key support in the SDK wrappers).
>
> I'll paste a minimal Node snippet below for your engineers to try. If you prefer Python we can send that too.
>
> --- sample (Node) ---
> const Redwood = require('redwood-sdk');
> const client = new Redwood.Client({ apiKey: process.env.RW_TEST_KEY, baseURL: 'https://sandbox.redwood.ai/v1' });
>
> async function send(req) {
>   const backoff = (attempt) => Math.min(3000, 200 * Math.pow(2, attempt));
>   for (let i=0;i<4;i++) {
>     try {
>       const res = await client.generate({ model: 'gpt-lite-1', prompt: req.prompt, timeout: 5000 });
>       return res;
>     } catch (err) {
>       if (err.status === 429) {
>         await sleep(backoff(i) + jitter());
>         continue;
>       }
>       if (err.status >= 500 && req.idempotent) {
>         await sleep(backoff(i));
>         continue;
>       }
>       throw err;
>     }
>   }
>   throw new Error('retry-exhausted');
> }
>
> --- end snippet ---
>
> We will push these snippets into a forkable repo and share the link. Also: yes, we provide scoped test keys and a sandbox project — Tessa will coordinate creating those for NovaCorp.
>
> Cheers,
> Marissa
